Dart OOP Concepts 
|================|

1). What is OOP in Dart?
-------------------------

OOP stands for object-Oriented Programming. 
It is a programming approach where we organize code using classes and objects. 

Ihstead of writing all application functionality in one place, we divide the application into smaller, reusable structures called classes.

1.1 Understanding Class and object

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Class                                           | object                                   |
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Blueprint or template                           | Instance of a class                      |
| Defines properties and methods                  | Uses properties and methods              |
| Does not represent an individual Instance       | Represents an actual Instance            |
| Example: User                                   | Example: user1                           |
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.2 How does OOP work in Dart?

A. Create a class
   Define the structure, properties and methods.

   class User{
    String name = 'Abhiram';
   }

B. Create an object
   Create an Instance using the class. 

   User user = User();

C. Access properties and methods
   use the dot( . ) operator.

   print(user.name);

D. Perform operations
   Call methods to perform the required functionality. 

   user.login();

1.3 Advantages and Disadvantages of OOP 

Advantages

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Advantages                    | Explanation                                                              |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Code reusability              | Reuse functionality instead of writing it repeatedly.                    |
| Maintainability               | Easier to modify and manage code                                         |
| Modularity                    | Divide large applications into smaller classes                           |
| Scalability                   | Easier to extend application functionality                               |
| Flexibility                   | Polymorphism allows different implementations through common interfaces  |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disadvantages

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Disadvantages                | Explanation                                                               |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Complexity                   | Beginners may find OOP concepts difficult                                 |
| More boilerplate             | Sometimes requires additional classes and structures                      |
| Overengineering              | Unnecessary classes and abstractions can make code complicated            |
| Learning curve               | Requires understanding multiple concepts                                  |
| Memory overhead              | Objects and their retained data consume memory                            |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.4 Four Pillars of OOP

A. 1. Encapsulation (Protect and control data)
B. 2. Inheritance (Reuse parent functionality)
C. 3. Abstraction (Hide implementation details)
D. 4. Polymorphism (One interface, different behaviours)

Pillar 1: Encapsulation
------------------------
Definition:

[Encapsulation is one of the four Pillars of Objects-Oriented Programming (OOP). It is the processes of wrapping data (variable) and methods (functions) together inside a class and restricting direct access to an object's internal data. 

In simple words:
Encapsulation means protecting the data inside a class and providing controlled  access to the data using methods, getter and setters.
]



Real-world example
Imagine a bank account.
You cannot directly change the account balance however you want. You should deposit or withdraw money through controlled operations.

class BankAccount {
    double _balance = 1000;

    double get balance =>  _balance;

    void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            _balance += amount;
        }
    }

    void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0 && amount <= _balance) {
            _balance -= amount;
        }
    }
}

void main() {
    final account = BankAccount();

    account.deposit(500);
    account.withdraw(200);

    print(account.balance);
}

1.1 Using a private variable
In dart, a variable or method starting with an underscore(_) is private to its library (Usually the Dart file).
Dart Privacy is library-level, not class-level. code in the same library can still access private. 

1.2 getter

A getter is a special method that allows us to read or retrieve the value of a private variable without directly exposing that variable. 
Getter = Get or Read the value. 

Syntax:
returnType get propertyName {
  return value;
}

1.3 setter

A setter is a special method that allows us to modify or update the value of a private variable with validation and controlled access.
Setter = Set or Update the value.

Syntax:
set propertyName(dataType value) {
  // Assign or validate value
}

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Getter                                       | Setter                               |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Used to read data                            | Used to modify data                  |
| Retrieves a value                            | Assigns a value                      |
| Does not require a parameter                 | Requires one parameter               |
| Has a return type                            | Has no return type                   |
| Uses get keyword                             | Uses set keyword                     |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Pillar 2: Inheritance
---------------------

Definition:

Inheritance is an OOP concept that allows a child class to acquire properties and methods from a parent class using the extends keyword.
In simple words: one class an reuse functionality from another class.

Use inheritance when there is a genuine parent-child or is-a relationship.


2.1 Parent and child classes

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Concepts                 | Meaning                                                   |
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Parent class             | Existing class whose properties and methods are inherited |
| Child Class              | 	New class that inherits from the parent class            |
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


class User {
    String name;

    User(this.name);

   void login(){
     print('$name logged in');
   }
}

class Employee extends User {
    Employee(super.name);

    void attendMeeting(){
      print('$name attending meeting');   
    }
}

void main(){
    final employee = Employee("Abhiram");

    employee.login();
    employee.attendMeeting();
}

Pillar 3: Abstraction
----------------------

Definition:

Abstraction is an OOP concept that hides internal implementation details and exposes only the essential functionality to the user.

In simple words: Show what something does, without exposing all the details of how it works.

Real-world example

When you use an ATM:
- Enter your PIN.
- Select withdraw
- Enter the amount
- Receive your money

You don't need to understand how the bank server processes the transaction internally. The ATM hides that complexity.

Dart example:

abstract class Payment {
  void pay(double amount);
}

class UpiPayment extends Payment {
  @override
  void pay(double amount) {
    print('Processing UPI payment: $amount');
  }
}

class CardPayment extends Payment {
  @override
  void pay(double amount) {
    print('Processing Card payment: $amount');
  }
}

void main() {
  final Payment payment = UpiPayment();

  payment.pay(500);
}

Pillar 4: Polymorphism
-----------------------
Definition:

[Polymorphism is an OOp concept that allow the same method or interface to have different implementation depending on the object using it. 

 In simple words:
 One method name can perform different actions for different objects. (One name, multiple behaviours.)
 The word Polymorphism comes from two Greek words:
 Poly = Many
 Morphism = Forms

]
class Payment {
  void makePayment() {
    print("Processing payment");
  }
}

class UpiPayment extends Payment {
 
  @override
  void makePayment() {
    print('Payment through UPI');
  }
}

class CardPayment extends Payment {

  @override
  void makePayment() {
    print('Payment through Credit Card');
  }
}


class NetBankingPayment extends Payment {

  @override
  void makePayment() {
    print('Payment through Net Banking');
  }
}

Important Advantages:
1. Code reusability - You can reuse common parent-class structures. 
2. Flexibility - Different objects can provide different implementations. 
3. Maintainability - You can change implementations without changing the common calling code. 
4. Extensibility - You can add new notification types without rewriting existing calling logic. 
5. Loose coupling - You code can depend on abstractions rather than specific implementations. 

4.1. Types of Polymorphism
A. Overloading (Compile-time/Static Polymorphism)
B. Overriding  (Runtime/Dynamic Polymorphism)

A. Compile-time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)
Definition:
Compile-time polymorphism is a form of polymorphism in which the method to be executed is determined during compilation based on the method signature or the arguments supplied.
[
  Method Overloading is the concept of defining multiple method with the same name but different parameter lists within the same class.
  Dart does not support traditional method Overloading with multiple method having the same name but different paramenter signatures. 

  How can we achieve similar functionality in Dart?
  1. Optional positional parameters
  2. Named optional parameters

  Important: These are ways to achieve similar flexible calling behaviour. They are not traditional method overloading because only one method declaration exists. 
]

B. Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)

Definition:
Runtime polymorphism is a form of polymorphism in which the method implementation that gets executed is determined at runtime based on the actual object being referenced.

[
  Method overriding occurs when a child class provides its own implementation of a method that is already defined in its parent class, using the same method name and a compatible signature.

  In simple words:
  A parent class defines a method, and a child class changes its implementation according to its requirements.

  Example:
  step 1: Create the parent class

  class Employee {
    void calculateSalary() {
      print('Calculating employee salary');
    }
  }

  Here:
  - Employee is the parent class.
  - calculateSalary() is the parent method.

  Step 2: Create child classes

  class Developer extends Employee {

   @override
   void calculateSalary() {
     print('Developer salary: 50000');
   }
  }

  class Manager extends Employee {

   @override
   void calculateSalary() {
     print('Manager salary: 80000');
   }
  }
]

4.2 What is the purpose of the @override annotation?
In Dart, @override is an annotation used to indicate that a method or member is intended to override an inherited member from a superclass or implement an interface member.


4.3 Method Overloading vs Method Overriding

Overloading = Same method name, different parameters.
Overriding = Same inherited method, different implementation.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Concept                  | 	Method Overloading                                        | Method Overriding                                          |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Meaning                  | Same method name with different parameter lists            | Child provides a new implementation of inherited method    |
| Classes                  | Traditionally within the same class                        |	Parent and child classes                                   |
| Parameters               | Different parameter lists                                  | Same or compatible signature                               |
| Inheritance              | Not required                                               | Required for class-based overriding                        |
| Polymorphism             | Compile-time (traditional classification)                  | Runtime                                                    |
| Dart support             | No traditional overloading                                 | Supported                                                  |
| Example                  | add(int, int) and add(int, int, int)                       | Animal.sound() and Dog.sound()                             |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 